// Unit u13: Objection Latin: the vocabulary of the exam.
// The terms (Latin, law French, English terms of art), the numbers, the rule numbers and the cases that stems and answer options
// assume you already know. Every term card carries its exam context, so vocabulary is always a rule. Written from primary text
// where the research read it and from standard black-letter law elsewhere; every example is our own. No NCBE or commercial text.
//
// Dropped on purpose: ultra vires (Business Associations: NextGen only, not on the MBE), SCREAM, BARRK, the G-SAM "S",
// Plyler's scrutiny tier, Roy Bean, 12 Angry Men, Better Call Saul (all flagged uncertain in research/hooks.md).
//
// STATUS NOTES FOR THE REVIEWER
//   book    : rule-number pairs whose headings were checked against the LII tables of contents on 2026-11-02 (FRE, FRCP, UCC Article 2);
//             the numbers read in the research files (28 U.S.C. §§1332, 1441-1447; FRCP deadlines; FRE 412, 413, 609, 803(16);
//             UCC 2-201, 2-205, 2-609, 2-702, 2-718, 2-725; USRAP 90 years; 12 U.S.C. §1701j-3(d)(4) re-read on LII 2026-11-02).
//   general : Latin and law French meanings and exam contexts (Wiktionary, Wikipedia list of Latin legal terms, standard doctrine);
//             case holdings known from summaries; the burden-of-proof sort; every Constitution item (article, amendment, veto and
//             pocket-veto numbers): the Constitution text was NOT re-read for this unit, so none of them is `book`.
//   unsure  : nothing. The two 2026 cards (Trump v. Slaughter, NRSC v. FEC) are `general` and `recent: true`: dates and outcomes
//             (Slaughter June 29, 2026, 6-3, Humphrey's Executor overruled as to the FTC; NRSC June 30, 2026, 6-3, Colorado II
//             overruled) come from several law-firm summaries, not the slip opinions. Before moving them to `book`, read both
//             opinions on supremecourt.gov. Slaughter did not reach non-Article III judges, and Trump v. Cook (Fed governor) went
//             the other way the same week, so the cards do not say "independent agencies are unconstitutional". NCBE questions lag
//             new cases by about three years, so none of this is probably scored in 2027. Chevron v. NRDC to Loper Bright (2024) was
//             dropped: administrative law is not an NCBE outline topic.
//
//   Points a reviewer should confirm even though the items are `general`:
//     - Texaco v. Pennzoil: the card teaches the Texas court of appeals' 1987 affirmance of tortious-interference liability (the
//       Houston jury verdict was November 1985, about $10.5 billion); the settlement figure differs between sources and is left out.
//       The U.S. Supreme Court's Pennzoil Co. v. Texaco Inc., 481 U.S. 1 (1987), is a different decision (Younger abstention, bond).
//     - Belton to Gant, Plessy to Brown, Korematsu to Trump v. Hawaii and Pennoyer to International Shoe are "overruled, abandoned or
//       narrowed" rather than formally overruled by name; the set title and the say lines say so.
//     - Carpenter's seven days: the Court held that seven days of historical cell-site records is a search and left shorter periods open.
//     - In loco parentis: the T.L.O. point (school officials are not mere parent stand-ins for Fourth Amendment purposes) is from memory.
//     - Rest. 3d Restitution §§31 and 36 (quantum meruit card): §39 was dropped (it is disgorgement for opportunistic breach); §31
//       was not re-read in the Restatement text.
//     - Vocabulary with no outline home (pro bono, pro hac vice, the three canons, per curiam, amicus curiae, a fortiori, inter alia,
//       bailment as a topic): kept because the brief lists them, filed under `skills` or flagged "background" in the `use` line, never
//       presented as an exam topic. Per stirpes / per capita sits closest to the Property class-gift material.
//   Not in NextGen's scope (flagged exam: ["mbe"]): the rule against perpetuities numbers (21 years, 90 years), class-action,
//   peremptory-challenge, new-trial, Rule 60, Rule 68 and jury-size numbers, the FRCP 23/47/49/51/52/60/68 rule numbers, voir dire.
//   Deliberately not built: a `num` card for FRE 807 notice and for common-law burglary "at night" (neither has a number; the 807
//   written-notice requirement and its good-cause exception sit in the FRE 807 pair), a Rule 1447(d) number card (same), and a typed
//   "Which rule?" drill (that game is generated from `rule` items only, which this cross-subject unit does not carry).
//
// DEEP VERIFICATION PASS 2026-11-02 (every changed item carries "corrected 2026-11-02" or "added 2026-11-02" in its src):
//   - Fixed: theft sort (land is not the subject of common-law theft: the widow row is now a chattel); embezzlement needs lawful
//     possession, not a trust; UCC remedy sort say (2-710 is the seller's); overruled sets (Roper moved to the narrowed set, Wayfair
//     added, title 2010-2019; say no longer names Belton); FRCP 15(a)(1) "whichever is earlier" reading; Blanton "presumptively
//     petty"; USRAP cited to the uniform act; 26(f) numbers tagged NextGen only (civpro.md §2); delegation row (the delegator stays
//     liable); incorporation say (no Seventh Amendment or grand jury); assumption of risk (pure comparative fault is the MBE
//     default); Shaffer/Burnham wording on in rem; mala prohibita (silent statutes presume a mental state); res ipsa covers causation;
//     sua sponte (personal jurisdiction is waivable, not beyond the court's notice); BFP under race-notice; scienter "reason to
//     know"; RAP for remainders vested subject to open; escrow relation back; statutory redemption; quantum meruit measure; ab
//     initio vs in pari delicto; case facts (Palsgraf guards, Hertz, Strawbridge, Sweatt, Dudley); Pennzoil filed under torts;
//     Pierson and res nullius flagged background (the MBE tests real property only).
//   - Restructured: lesson 1 interleaves its three pairs sets and adds a criminal/civil/either sort; the low-yield civpro.md §3
//     deadlines moved to u13l7 "Background numbers" (say lines start "Background:"); six non-brief story cases moved to u13l8.
//     Lessons 2 and 3 were reordered so no sitting ends with a lone item.
//   - Added: feoffment (history only), novation, accord and satisfaction, M'Naghten, subpoena duces tecum, a concurrent-ownership
//     set, right of entry and executory interest rows, and Gant and Crawford case cards.
//   - Trump v. Slaughter: press reports (June 29-30, 2026) agree on 6-3 and Humphrey's overruled; still not read on supremecourt.gov,
//     so it stays `general` + recent. The Trump v. Cook sentence was dropped from its say.
